# Thales' theorem
subtitle: "Why any angle drawn in a semicircle is exactly ninety degrees, proved with nothing more than three equal radii."
thumb: chalkboard

## Thales' theorem
use: geometry_figure
with:
    kind: circle_theorem
    vertex_labels:
      - C
      - A
      - B
    angle_labels:
      - 90°

    shape centre #8a5fa8 shape circle x 500 y 375 radius 6 style.fill #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label l_centre "O" below #8a5fa8 attach_to centre hidden true
    path rc #8a5fa8 points [[500,375],[358.59,543.53]] style.stroke_width 3.5 hidden true
    text l_rc "r" #8a5fa8 x 437 y 425 font_size 26 hidden true
    path ra #8a5fa8 points [[500,375],[280,375]] style.stroke_width 3.5 hidden true
    text l_ra "r" #8a5fa8 x 400 y 352 font_size 26 hidden true
    path rb #8a5fa8 points [[500,375],[720,375]] style.stroke_width 3.5 hidden true
    text l_rb "r" #8a5fa8 x 600 y 352 font_size 26 hidden true
    angle a_A #b5482e at [280,375] from 0 to 65 radius 70 label "a = 65°" hidden true
    angle a_CA #b5482e at [358.59,543.53] from 245 to 310 radius 60 label a hidden true
    angle a_B #2f6b8f at [720,375] from 155 to 180 radius 110 label "b = 25°" hidden true
    angle a_CB #2f6b8f at [358.59,543.53] from 310 to 335 radius 95 label b hidden true
    math sum tex 2a+2b=180^\circ size 34 colors.a #b5482e colors.b #2f6b8f x 850 y 600 hidden true
    path c2_a dashed thick #8f887c points [[280,375],[610,565.53]] hidden true
    path c2_b dashed thick #8f887c points [[610,565.53],[720,375]] hidden true
    angle a_C2 #8f887c at [610,565.53] from 210 to 300 radius 34 hidden true
    group other_c members ["c2_a","c2_b","a_C2"] hidden true
    path d_a dashed thick #b5482e points [[280,375],[500,480]] hidden true
    path d_b dashed thick #b5482e points [[500,480],[720,375]] hidden true
    angle a_D #b5482e at [500,480] from 205.5 to 334.5 radius 34 hidden true
    group inside members ["d_a","d_b","a_D","o_a","o_b","a_O"] hidden true
    text q "90° ?" #3d3833 x 330 y 590 font_size 28 hidden true
    path o_a dashed thick #2f6b8f points [[280,375],[500,650]] hidden true
    path o_b dashed thick #2f6b8f points [[500,650],[720,375]] hidden true
    angle a_O #2f6b8f at [500,650] from 231.3 to 308.7 radius 40 hidden true

    card title "Thales' theorem"

Why is an angle drawn in a semicircle always exactly ninety degrees? Start with a circle and a diameter from A to B.
    draw circle side_0 vertex_label_1 vertex_label_2

Pick a point C on the circle and join it to both ends. Wherever C sits, this angle measures ninety degrees. But why?
    draw side_2 side_1 vertex_label_0 q

The key is the centre, O. Draw one more line, from O to C: it is a radius.
    draw centre l_centre rc l_rc

O A and O B are radii too. Three equal lengths, and they split the big triangle into two smaller ones.
    draw ra l_ra rb l_rb

Take the left one, A O C. Two of its sides are radii, so it is isosceles.
    focus ra rc side_2 l_ra l_rc

An isosceles triangle has equal base angles: here sixty-five degrees at A, and the same sixty-five at C. Call it a.
    draw a_A a_CA

The right one, B O C, is isosceles too: twenty-five degrees at B and twenty-five at C. Call it b.
    draw a_B a_CB

Now the whole triangle A B C. Its angles are a at A, b at B, and a plus b at C, and they add to 180.
    draw sum

Halve both sides: a plus b is ninety. And a plus b is exactly the angle at C.
    morph sum tex a+b=90^\circ duration 1.5

    erase q

So the angle at C is a right angle, forced by nothing more than three equal radii.
    draw angle_0 angle_label_0

Move C anywhere else on the circle and the base angles change, but they always pair up the same way: ninety again.
    draw other_c

    erase other_c

Off the circle, it fails. From a point inside, the diameter looks wider: 129 degrees here. From outside, narrower: 77.
    draw inside

The story goes that Thales, around 600 BC, sacrificed an ox when he found this: it is among the first theorems anyone is said to have proved.
    erase inside

    quiz "C moves so that its angle at A becomes 40°. What is the angle at B?"
        right: Fifty degrees: the base angles still pair up at C, so the angles at A and B add to ninety wherever C sits.
        * 40°
        + 50°
        * 90°
---
